[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mNext fork: where the "real user id" comes from before the server exists.[0m This one has a trap in it, so I want to be
  direct about it.

  A browser can't know who someone is. Anything we mint client-side identifies a [3mbrowser profile[0m, not a person — two
  people sharing a laptop get one id, one person on phone and desktop gets two. Calling that [38;5;153muserId[39m writes a false
  promise into the name, and the bill comes due at cutover, when the server has actual user ids and something has to
  reconcile them.

  - [1mMint an opaque id, name it honestly[0m ([38;5;153mdeviceId[39m or [38;5;153mclientId[39m), store it, and send it with the login attempt. The server
    later returns the real [38;5;153muserId[39m and the identity module starts preferring it, keeping the device id as a correlation
    key. Nothing has to be un-taught later because nothing was misnamed.
  - [1mUse the username as the id.[0m Stable, readable, zero plumbing — and it puts a user-supplied string in storage as a
    key, breaks if usernames are ever editable, and is the thing most likely to collide with the server's real id
    scheme.
  - [1mLeave it null until the server exists.[0m Build the identity module and the log, have [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m return null for now.
    Maximally honest, but every consumer has to handle null from day one and you get no tracking in the meantime — which
    is what you asked for.

  I recommend the first: mint an opaque id, but name it for what it is. The identity module exposes both — a device id
  available now, and a user id that stays null until the backend fills it — so "other forms" code against the real shape
  from the start instead of against a placeholder that lies.
